 ],
 "notes": "From the garden views (a long, low single-storey house under thin flat dark roofs with warm wood soffits; the social wing raised on a plinth with a five-metre gallery wrapping its south and east sides on W310 steel, glass walls under a concrete band; the lower bedroom wing with a concrete chimney box, set partly into the slope), the approach views (rubble field-stone walls either side of a glass entrance hall under a concrete lintel, steps down from the road between tussocks of feather grass, a horizontal window in the stone), the carob tree preserved in a cobbled patio with a concrete edge just south of the hall, the raised pool with a corten side and travertine coping, grass terraces with concrete edges, and a carport under the same roof type. This pass: the carob is now the largest thing on the site after the roofs - an umbrella crown with drooping lobes hanging lower all round (photo 01: its foliage weeps nearly to head height and over the gallery), a thick trunk splitting into leaning limbs, over a patio of rubble cobbles in a concrete kerb, with the timber bench on the gallery's concrete plinth face beside it; the native trees round it are real umbrella trees, smaller and airier than before, so the carob reads as the origin; the stone walls are grey-brown rubble as photographed, not dressed stone; the social wing's plinth is rubble with concrete coping and a concrete face to the patio; the pool is a raised pool (water at 0.55 m) in a corten tank with travertine coping; concrete edges on the terrace steps; feather-grass tussocks (green and straw) along the approach and round the hall. Still guessed: orientation (road north, garden south; social wing east, per the article's 'social to the left'); plan dimensions; the carport's and pool's positions; the long free-standing rubble retaining wall seen across the lawn in two photos, which could not be placed and is left out; the gallery roof's single pitch is too slight to draw and is flat; the site's slope is flattened except for the social wing's plinth."
}